@*
    Ask CircleAI.

    A COPY OF MainActivity + ChatView: a quiet status line, the turns as bubbles -
    yours solid blue on the right, its bordered surface on the left - and a
    composer pinned to the bottom.

    THE BORDER ON THE REPLY MATTERS. On a dark background an unbordered card and
    the page behind it are the same colour at a glance, and the reply stops looking
    like a distinct thing.
*@
@page "/chat"
@inject IBrain Brain
@inject IFormFactor Host
@inject IConversation Talk
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<div class="chat">
    @*
        THE STATE GOES WHERE THE EYE IS, and only once there is a conversation to
        sit above.

        An empty chat put a line of small grey text at the very top of the screen
        and left the fourteen hundred pixels below it blank - a page with nothing
        in its middle and everything crushed against the bottom edge. And the line
        it printed was an instruction, "answering needs a 548 MB download, turn it
        on under Settings › Phone", with nothing to press: the one screen that
        knows exactly what is missing made the person go and find it.
    *@
    @if (_turns.Count > 0 && _status.Length > 0)
    {
        <div class="status">@_status</div>
    }

    <div class="turns">
        @if (_turns.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="empty">
                <BrandMark Size="96" Ring="true" />

                @if (_ready)
                {
                    <div class="empty-line">Ask it something</div>
                    @* TAP, NOT HOLD. It said "hold the button to talk", and the
                       button takes one turn on a single press - describing a
                       gesture the app does not have is how somebody decides the
                       microphone is broken. *@
                    <div class="empty-sub">Type below, or tap the circle to talk.</div>
                }
                else
                {
                    <div class="empty-line">Not ready yet</div>
                    <div class="empty-sub">@_status</div>

                    @*
                        THE QUESTION SURVIVES THE WAIT.

                        A tile in Services exists to get somebody past the blank
                        page - it arrives carrying a first sentence, which the
                        composer holds ready to send. With no answering model the
                        composer is not drawn, so that sentence was silently
                        dropped: somebody tapped "How I feel", landed on "not
                        ready yet", and nothing on the screen remembered what they
                        had come to ask. After a 548 MB download they would have
                        to remember it themselves.

                        It is still in the box the moment there is a box.
                    *@
                    @if (_draft.Length > 0)
                    {
                        <div class="kept">
                            <span class="kept-label">You wanted to ask</span>
                            <span class="kept-text">@_draft</span>
                        </div>
                    }

                    @*
                        THE INSTRUCTION, MADE PRESSABLE - but only where pressing
                        it does something. On the phone this is a download waiting
                        to be started; in a browser the message is "install the
                        app to have a conversation", and a button offering to turn
                        that on would be a button that cannot.
                    *@
                    @if (Host.IsOnDevice)
                    {
                        <a class="empty-go" href="settings">Turn it on</a>
                    }
                }
            </div>
        }

        @foreach (var (turn, i) in _turns.Select((t, i) => (t, i)))
        {
            <div class="row @(turn.Mine ? "row-mine" : "row-its")" @key="i">
                <div class="bubble @(turn.Mine ? "mine" : "its")">@turn.Text</div>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    @*
        WHAT THIS SCREEN CAN DO TO THE CONVERSATION, and nothing else.

        There were two more controls here: "What it can do", which went to the
        marketing screen, and a full-width blue "Speak in 10 plus languages",
        which went to the language list. Both are destinations, both are already
        on the bottom bar or a tile in Services, and the blue one was the most
        prominent control on a screen whose actual job is Send.

        Home carries the rule in its own words - "a screen that offers the same
        three destinations twice is a screen arguing with its own navigation" -
        and dropped those exact links when the bar arrived. This screen kept them.

        What is left acts on the conversation rather than leaving it: read a
        picture into it, and read the last answer out loud. Both belong to the
        turns above them and exist nowhere else.
    *@
    @*
        ONLY WITH A CONVERSATION TO ACT ON.

        Both of these act on turns that exist: one reads a picture INTO the
        transcript, the other reads the last answer out of it - and with no
        answer yet, "Read it out" is a disabled button taking up a row. Empty,
        the screen was three strips with voids between them: a small block adrift
        in the middle, a dead toolbar, then the composer.
    *@
    @if (_turns.Count > 0)
    {
    <div class="probes">
        <label class="probe">
            Read an image
            <InputFile OnChange="OnImage" accept="image/*" hidden />
        </label>
        <button class="probe" @onclick="Speak" disabled="@(_busy || LastReply is null)">
            Read it out
        </button>
    </div>
    }

    @*
        ONE FIELD, NOT THREE CONTROLS IN A ROW.

        A grey rounded box, a grey square with a microphone in it and a blue
        rectangle sat side by side as three different shapes doing one job, which
        is most of what made this page look a decade old. They are one rounded
        field now, with the two buttons inside it.

        THE MIC STAYS even though the bar has a bigger one 33 pixels below. They
        are not the same thing: the bar's button takes a spoken turn wherever you
        are, and this one puts what was heard and what was answered into the
        transcript above it. Losing that would be losing the only way a spoken
        turn joins the conversation you are reading.
    *@
    @*
        NO BOX TO TYPE IN WHEN NOTHING CAN READ IT.

        The composer was rendered disabled: a field you cannot type in, a mic that
        does nothing and a greyed Send, sitting under a message explaining that the
        answering model has not been downloaded. Three dead controls and the gap
        under them, on a screen with one thing to say and one button to press.

        With it gone the not-ready screen is a single column with one thing in it,
        which is what "use the space" means here - not spreading three strips out
        to fill the height.

        Kept when turns exist even if the brain has since gone away, because a
        conversation you can read and not continue still needs its box in place
        rather than vanishing under you.
    *@
    @if (_ready || _turns.Count > 0)
    {
    <div class="composer">
        <div class="composer-field">
            @* Same as the CV answer box: a controlled value re-applied on
               every render drops characters while somebody is typing. *@
            <input class="input" placeholder="Type a message"
                   aria-label="Your message"
                   disabled="@(!_ready)"
                   @bind="_draft" @bind:event="oninput"
                   @onkeydown="OnKey" />

            <button class="mic" @onclick="Listen" disabled="@(!_ready || _busy)"
                    aria-label="Use the mic">🎙</button>
            <button class="send" @onclick="Send" disabled="@(!_ready || _busy)">Send</button>
        </div>
    </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    readonly List<Utterance> _turns = [];
    string _draft = "";
    string _status = "Getting ready…";
    bool _ready;
    bool _busy;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var state = await Brain.StateAsync();
        _ready = state.Ready;

        // A TILE ARRIVES AS A QUESTION, not as a category. The grid's whole point
        // is getting somebody past the blank page, so the opener is put in the box
        // rather than sent - they can edit it, which somebody nudged into a
        // subject usually wants to.
        //
        // Parsed by hand rather than with QueryHelpers: that lives in
        // Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities, which this Razor library does not
        // reference, and one query parameter is not worth a package.
        var q = new Uri(Nav.Uri).Query;
        const string key = "ask=";
        var at = q.IndexOf(key, StringComparison.Ordinal);
        if (at >= 0)
        {
            var value = q[(at + key.Length)..];
            var amp = value.IndexOf('&');
            if (amp >= 0) value = value[..amp];
            _draft = Uri.UnescapeDataString(value);
        }

        // The reason, not "unavailable": "Answering needs a 548 MB model" tells
        // somebody what to do next.
        _status = state.Ready ? "" : state.Detail;
    }

    async Task OnKey(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Enter") await Send();
    }

    async Task Send()
    {
        var text = _draft.Trim();
        if (text.Length == 0 || _busy || !_ready) return;

        _turns.Add(new Utterance(true, text));
        _draft = "";
        _busy = true;

        // The open reply, appended to as fragments arrive.
        var reply = new Utterance(false, "");
        _turns.Add(reply);
        var at = _turns.Count - 1;

        try
        {
            await Brain.AskAsync(text, fragment =>
            {
                _turns[at] = _turns[at] with { Text = Strip(_turns[at].Text + fragment) };
                InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
            });
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _turns[at] = _turns[at] with { Text = $"{ex.GetType().Name}: {ex.Message}" };
        }
        finally
        {
            // AN EMPTY BUBBLE MEANS THE MODEL PRODUCED NOTHING. Leaving it is a
            // grey rectangle with no explanation, which reads as a bug rather
            // than as silence.
            if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_turns[at].Text)) _turns.RemoveAt(at);
            _busy = false;
        }
    }

    string? LastReply => _turns.LastOrDefault(t => !t.Mine)?.Text;

    /// <summary>Speak the last answer aloud.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The app is a VOICE assistant, and a chat screen that can only be read is
    /// the demo rather than the product - especially for somebody who cannot read
    /// comfortably, who is a large part of who this is for.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task Speak()
    {
        if (LastReply is not { Length: > 0 } text) return;
        _busy = true;
        try { await Talk.SayAsync(text); }
        catch (Exception ex) { _status = $"{ex.GetType().Name}: {ex.Message}"; }
        finally { _busy = false; }
    }

    /// <summary>One spoken turn: listen, answer, say it back.</summary>
    async Task Listen()
    {
        if (_busy) return;
        _busy = true;

        var updates = new Progress<TurnState>(t =>
        {
            _status = t.Phase switch
            {
                TurnPhase.Listening => "Listening…",
                TurnPhase.Thinking => "Working on it…",
                TurnPhase.Speaking => "Answering…",
                _ => t.Detail ?? "",
            };

            // The heard question and the reply become turns as soon as they exist,
            // so the conversation reads the same whether it was typed or spoken.
            if (t.Heard is { Length: > 0 } heard
                && _turns.LastOrDefault(x => x.Mine)?.Text != heard)
                _turns.Add(new Utterance(true, heard));

            if (t.Reply is { Length: > 0 } reply)
            {
                if (_turns.Count > 0 && !_turns[^1].Mine) _turns[^1] = new Utterance(false, reply);
                else _turns.Add(new Utterance(false, reply));
            }

            InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        });

        try { await Talk.TurnAsync(updates); }
        catch (Exception ex) { _status = $"{ex.GetType().Name}: {ex.Message}"; }
        finally { _busy = false; }
    }

    /// <summary>Answer a question about a picture.</summary>
    async Task OnImage(InputFileChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        if (_busy) return;
        _busy = true;
        _status = "Looking at it…";

        try
        {
            // Capped: a modern phone camera produces files a vision model has no
            // use for at full size, and reading one into memory on a P30 is how a
            // screen dies rather than answers.
            await using var stream = e.File.OpenReadStream(8 * 1024 * 1024);
            using var buffer = new MemoryStream();
            await stream.CopyToAsync(buffer);

            _turns.Add(new Utterance(true, $"What is in this picture? ({e.File.Name})"));
            var reply = new Utterance(false, "");
            _turns.Add(reply);
            var at = _turns.Count - 1;

            await Brain.SeeAsync("What is in this picture?", buffer.ToArray(), fragment =>
            {
                _turns[at] = _turns[at] with { Text = _turns[at].Text + fragment };
                InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
            });

            if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_turns[at].Text)) _turns.RemoveAt(at);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _status = $"{ex.GetType().Name}: {ex.Message}";
        }
        finally
        {
            _status = "";
            _busy = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Removes the routing marker the session streams in front of its answers.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The session prefixes replies with something like "IT! &gt; ", which made
    /// sense when every turn was a line in one text buffer and you needed to know
    /// who was talking. A bubble already says that by its side and its colour, so
    /// the prefix is the old interface leaking into the new one - and left alone,
    /// the first thing the assistant appears to say is its own name and a piece of
    /// punctuation.
    /// <para>
    /// Chunks arrive a few characters at a time, so the marker can be split across
    /// several. Re-checked while the text is still short rather than tried once.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    static string Strip(string text)
    {
        if (text.Length > 24) return text;
        var cut = text.IndexOf('>');
        return cut >= 0 && cut <= 12 && text[..cut].Trim().Length <= 10
            ? text[(cut + 1)..].TrimStart()
            : text;
    }
}
